I've made 943 serious mistakes in my life. I'm documenting every one of them so you don't have to make the same ones. My name is Lou Zerr. Here's another 4 of 943 (part 2 of 7).\par\par\par
The Wednesday I Made Eggs Instead\par\par\par
The line moved a full point between when I woke up and when the eggs were done.\par\par\par
The line moved a full point between when I woke up and when the eggs were done.\par\par\par
I didn't know that then. I know it now because I went back and looked at the historical line data, which is a thing you can do for free, which is a thing I could have done any time all week. On Wednesday at 6:47 a.m., which is roughly when I was cracking two eggs into a nonstick pan, the home team was minus 2. By 7:15, when I sat down to eat, it was minus 2 and dropping.\par\par\par
My team was leaking value while I salted my breakfast.\par\par\par
But I want to describe the Wednesday morning honestly, because this is where the arc actually happens. Not Tuesday night when I placed the bet. Not Sunday afternoon when I lost it. Wednesday morning. That was the day I had every chance to catch it, and instead I did something that felt more responsible than looking at my phone.\par\par\par
I made breakfast.\par\par\par
Here is the small self-portrait. Kitchen light on, the yellow one over the sink because the overhead was still too bright for 6:45. Coffee already gurgling. I had my reading glasses pushed up on my forehead because I was going to check the paper \emdash  actual paper, delivered, don't laugh \emdash  after I ate. The dog was staring at me from her spot by the fridge doing the guilt thing.\par\par\par
My phone was on the counter, face down. I remember this specifically because I remember thinking, look at me. Look at me being present. Look at me not being one of these guys who wakes up and immediately doomscrolls. My wife had been on me about phone-first mornings and I had decided, sometime in the last week, to be better about it.\par\par\par
So I made the eggs. I toasted the bread. I ate. I read the front page and the sports section. I let the dog out. I let the dog in. I put the plate in the dishwasher. I did all of this in the specific self-congratulatory mode of a man who thinks not looking at his phone for thirty minutes is a personality upgrade.\par\par\par
And the line kept moving.\par\par\par
Here's what I want to say and it's going to sound like I'm being hard on myself but I'm not, I'm just being accurate. There is a version of me \emdash  a version that exists in every other functional adult I know \emdash  that would have, at some point during that Wednesday morning, opened the sportsbook app for ten seconds. Not to bet. Not to obsess. Just to see. Just to confirm the world hadn't moved.\par\par\par
But I had put the phone face down. And in my head, checking the app would have violated the tiny virtue I had built for myself that morning. It would have been backsliding. It would have been the phone-first behavior my wife had complained about.\par\par\par
So I made the mistake dressed as a good habit.\par\par\par
That's the part I keep chewing on. Not that I didn't check. That I didn't check because I was performing wellness. I traded ninety seconds of information for the story of being a man who eats breakfast without a screen.\par\par\par
I got in the car at 7:40. Drove to work. Radio, not phone. Another small virtue. Got to the office at 8:05. Coffee, small talk, opened my laptop, opened email, opened a spreadsheet, opened another spreadsheet. Never once, in any of those app-openings, did I open the sportsbook.\par\par\par
By noon on Wednesday the line had settled at minus 2. That's a full point of movement in eighteen hours. In sharp-money language that's the market shouting.\par\par\par
I was in a meeting about Q3 targets.\par\par\par
I want to tell you I felt something in the back of my mind, some tug, some suspicion. I didn't. That's the honest part. The bet had left my mind entirely by lunch. I had done the hard work Tuesday. That was the whole point of doing it Tuesday.\par\par\par
Wednesday, in my head, was a day off.\par\par\par
Wednesday, in reality, was the day the market started to tell me I was wrong. And I was making eggs. And I was proud of the eggs.\par\par\par
By Friday I actually did open the app. And I still didn't see it. Wait until you hear why.\par\par\par
\emdash  Lou\par\par\par
